<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Academic Achievement and Skill Development – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development: How?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze informational content for specific data. Reading: Make basic inferences or predictions about a text content from headings, titles, or headlines.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze informational texts about academic success to find specific study tips and strategies (e.g., "The article says that we should create a study schedule to manage our time better."). Can make predictions about a text's content based on its title or headings (e.g., "From the title 'Top 5 Study Tips for Success,' I think the article will suggest ideas like setting goals or taking breaks."). Can infer the purpose of a section in a study guide by analyzing headings and key phrases (e.g., "The heading 'Time Management Strategies' suggests this section will explain how to organize study time effectively.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e6e6e6; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images related to academic success (e.g., a student receiving an award, a well-organized study desk, students collaborating). Students work in pairs to discuss: "What do these images suggest about academic achievement and skill development? What strategies do you think lead to success?" The teacher prompts students to use vocabulary like 'goals,' 'effort,' 'discipline,' and 'improvement.' For example, "What kind of effort is shown here for improvement?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher presents the title of an article: "Unlock Your Potential: Top Strategies for Academic Success." The teacher explicitly models how to make predictions from the title and headings. "From this title, I predict the article will give us advice on how to improve our grades and develop new study skills. It might suggest things like time management or creating a study plan." The teacher models the pronunciation of 'potential' and 'strategy' while emphasizing stress patterns for words like 'progress' (noun) and 'progress' (verb).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What type of information do you expect to find in an article with 'Top Strategies' in its title?" (Expected answer: Advice, tips, ways to improve.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it useful to predict content before reading?" (Expected answer: Helps understand better, activates prior knowledge, prepares us.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Authentic informational article/blog post titled "Unlock Your Potential: Top Strategies for Academic Success" with subheadings such as "Effective Time Management," "Active Learning and Engagement," and "Seeking Feedback and Growth." The text explicitly integrates target vocabulary (e.g., 'achievement,' 'skill,' 'development,' 'advice,' 'study,' 'goals,' 'improvement,' 'practice,' 'focus,' 'feedback,' 'growth,' 'strategy,' 'effort,' 'progress,' 'plan,' 'discipline,' 'success,' 'time management,' 'motivation') and grammar (modals for advice, second/first conditionals) from the curriculum.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Can analyze informational content for specific data and make basic inferences or predictions about a text content from headings, titles, or headlines.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students first read the article's title and subheadings. In pairs, they predict what kind of advice each section will provide. For example, "From 'Effective Time Management,' I think the article will suggest creating a study schedule."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Skimming for Main Ideas:</b> Students quickly read the entire article to identify the main topic of each section. They match each subheading to a brief summary statement provided by the teacher (e.g., "This section explains how to organize your study time" goes with "Effective Time Management").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False based on general understanding:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements about the overall message of the article (e.g., "This article mainly focuses on physical exercise for academic success. T/F"). Students answer individually and then check with a partner.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific information, identifying advice, and using modals (should, must, can) and second conditional to discuss hypothetical advice. Vocabulary practice includes 'strategy,' 'discipline,' 'motivation,' 'procrastinate,' 'review,' 'organize,' 'achieve,' 'reflect.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Information Extraction:</b> Students re-read the article more carefully. They complete a graphic organizer or table with two columns: "Study Strategy" and "Benefit/Purpose." For example, under "Effective Time Management," they might write "Create a study schedule" and "Manage time better and avoid procrastination." This activity helps them analyze informational content for specific data.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Advice Application:</b> The teacher provides sentence stems based on the article's advice, incorporating modals and conditional structures. Students complete them using information from the text or their own ideas inspired by the text.
                        <br>Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"According to the article, you ______ (should/must/can) set achievable goals to track your progress."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I wanted to improve my grades, I ______ (would) focus more on active learning, as the article suggests."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you ______ (practice) regularly, you ______ (will) develop strong study skills." (First conditional)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Discussing Challenges and Solutions:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives them short scenarios about common academic challenges (e.g., "My friend struggles with remembering information after studying," or "I often procrastinate on my assignments."). Students read the scenarios and then use the advice from the article to suggest solutions, employing modals for advice and the second conditional. For example: "If your friend were having trouble remembering, they should try the active recall strategy from the article." or "If I were better at time management, I wouldn't procrastinate as much."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will collaboratively create a "Study Tips Guide" for new 10th-grade students based on the article and their own insights. This task encourages them to synthesize information from the reading and apply it.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small groups (3-4 students) will be assigned one section of the original article (e.g., "Effective Time Management," "Active Learning," "Seeking Feedback"). They will re-read their assigned section carefully, identify the most important advice, and then formulate 3-4 key study tips using clear, actionable language, including modals for advice (e.g., "You should," "You must," "You can"). They must also explain why each tip is important (its benefit). For example, "You should create a study schedule; if you do this, you will manage your time effectively and avoid procrastination."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group will then present their "Top Tips" to the class, making sure to use inclusive and encouraging language (e.g., "Let's consider implementing..."). They will also practice stress patterns for words like 'progress' (noun) when talking about achieving "academic progress."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This activity focuses on fluency in expressing advice and synthesizing information from reading, linking directly to the theme of "Academic Achievement and Skill Development: How?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and presentation, focusing on students' ability to analyze the text, articulate advice, and use target language. A quick peer assessment during presentations to check comprehension of advice given by other groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will write down one piece of advice from another group's presentation that they found most useful, explaining why. They must use either a modal for advice or a second conditional structure. For example, "I think we should all try to seek feedback regularly, because if we did, we would see significant growth in our skills."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of the advice provided.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'strategy,' 'focus,' 'discipline,' 'improvement,' 'achievement').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of modals for advice (should, must, can) and/or second conditional for hypothetical situations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated ability to infer the purpose of a section from its advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legible writing and appropriate sentence structure.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new study strategy I learned today from the reading is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I applied this strategy, I would expect to improve my..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategies did I use today to understand the article better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now in analyzing informational texts to find specific advice?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading activities effectively engaged students in analyzing content and making inferences. The use of graphic organizers and sentence stems proved helpful for accuracy. Some students might still confuse the noun 'progress' with the verb 'progress' in pronunciation; this will require more targeted practice. The group presentations allowed for good communicative practice, but some groups needed more guidance on formulating clear advice. For the next lesson, I will ensure specific speaking prompts encourage the use of the second conditional for giving advice to address this. I also need to provide more explicit feedback on the pronunciation of contrastive sounds like /gr/ and /kr/ during speaking activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), students will orally discuss their own academic challenges and give each other advice using the strategies and language practiced today. Understanding these reading strategies will be crucial for effective peer-to-peer communication about academic improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>